  "generated_text": "To find the probability that George flips exactly 2 tails in 7 flips, we can use the binomial probability formula. The binomial probability formula is given by:\n\n$P(X=k) = \\binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trials, $k$ is the number of successes, and $p$ is the probability of success.\n\nIn this case, $n=7$, $k=2$, and $p=\\frac{3}{4}$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we get:\n\n$P(X=2) = \\binom{7}{2} \\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^5$\n\nSimplifying this expression, we get:\n\n$P(X=2) = 21 \\cdot \\frac{9}{16} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1024}$\n\n$P(X=2) = \\frac{189}{16384}$\n\nSo the probability that George flips exactly 2 tails is $\\boxed{\\frac{189}{16384}}$.",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[189/16384, '\\\\frac{189}{16384}']",
    "gold_answer": "The probability that two coins land tails and five coins land heads, in some order, is $\\left( \\frac{3}{4} \\right)^2 \\left( \\frac{1}{4} \\right)^5=\\frac{9}{16384}$. Any two of the seven coins can be tails, so this can happen in $\\binom{7}{2}=21$ ways. Therefore, the probability that exactly two coins come up tails is $21 \\cdot \\frac{9}{16384}=\\boxed{\\frac{189}{16384}}$.",
    "is_correct": true
  },
